# ast-grep structural search

Use this guidance to translate a natural-language code query into an ast-grep pattern or YAML rule, test it against representative snippets, inspect syntax when needed, and search the intended contained project scope.

## Provenance and authority

This file synchronizes the official [ast-grep agent skill](https://github.com/ast-grep/agent-skill) and its rule reference at revision `c2a9bc154f4ffe08b25d28d5e852dfac8c0d0d8a`, retrieved 2026-08-03.

The root `AGENTS.md` is the shared cross-agent contract: Codex discovers it directly, while Claude Code consumes it through the `CLAUDE.md` import. Independently authored, applicable Claude project rules add native scoped guidance; `.codex/rules/` contains only Codex command-execution policy. In particular, inspect files, run tools, probe the installed ast-grep 0.45.0 binary, and resolve discoverable facts before presenting a result. Do not ask a user for information that repository or tool inspection can determine.

ast-soleaux structural-search tools are read-only. Rewrites and rule fixes may be previewed, but structural tools never apply them.

## Workflow

1. Determine the target language, code structure, intended scope, and inclusions or exclusions from repository evidence and the request.
2. Create minimal positive and negative snippets that cover the requested behavior.
3. Start with the simplest structural pattern or rule that can express the query.
4. Call `find_code` directly for a pattern; parse failures return a syntax diagnostic without a separate dump call.
5. Call `find_code_by_rule` with `positive_code` and `negative_code` so rule validation and project search complete in one call.
6. Use `dump_syntax_tree` only when node kinds or pattern parsing remain uncertain after the structured diagnostic.
7. Simplify a failing rule, verify each part, and then rebuild composite or relational constraints.
8. Use `scan_project_rules` only for rules retained from the startup configuration.
9. Verify representative returned matches and report the actual bounded result envelope.

Use `outline_code` with canonical exact paths or bounded `include_globs` before a broad search when repository structure or symbol names are unknown. Never guess paths; inspect `resolved_paths` and `path_errors`. For contained JavaScript or TypeScript dependency edges, use `oxc_modules` only when `get_server_info.capabilities.javascript_module_inspection` is true. Use `get_server_info` before choosing an uncertain language or when roots, configuration, capabilities, coordinates, or limits matter.

## Choosing an interface

| Need | Tool |
| --- | --- |
| Inspect parser structure or a parsed pattern | `dump_syntax_tree` |
| Test an inline YAML rule against a snippet | `test_match_code_rule` |
| Discover symbols in explicit files | `outline_code` |
| Search with one structural pattern | `find_code` |
| Search with one or more inline YAML rules | `find_code_by_rule` |
| Resolve contained JavaScript or TypeScript module edges | `oxc_modules` |
| Inspect one bounded TypeScript Compiler API project snapshot | `inspect_typescript_project` |
| Parse, scan, fingerprint, or inspect PL/pgSQL | `postgres_parse` |
| Apply the same PostgreSQL operations to contained files | `postgres_parse_files` |
| Preview PostgreSQL deparse with round-trip proof | `postgres_deparse_preview` |
| Run startup-retained project rules | `scan_project_rules` |
| Run startup-retained rule tests | `test_project_rules` |
| Inspect the effective server contract | `get_server_info` |

Use `find_code` for a direct single-node pattern. Use `find_code_by_rule` when the query needs relational rules, composite logic, constraints, transformations, metadata, or a rule-level fix preview. TypeScript compiler and PostgreSQL parser questions belong to their dedicated tools rather than being approximated through unsupported ast-grep language patterns; interactive definitions, references, hover, and connected PostgreSQL semantics remain owned by `lsp-soleaux`.

## Rule document

A searchable YAML rule normally has this shape:

```yaml
id: async-with-await
language: JavaScript
severity: info
message: async function containing await
rule:
  kind: function_declaration
  has:
    pattern: await $EXPR
    stopBy: end
```

Fields in the `rule` object are implicitly combined with logical AND. At least one positive matcher such as `pattern`, `kind`, or a positive composite rule is required. Use an explicit ordered `all` when a later sub-rule depends on metavariables captured by an earlier sub-rule.

## Atomic rules

| Property | Purpose | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pattern` | Match code structure | `pattern: console.log($ARG)` |
| `kind` | Match a tree-sitter node kind | `kind: call_expression` |
| `regex` | Match the full node text with Rust regex | `regex: ^[a-z]+$` |
| `nthChild` | Match a 1-based sibling position | `nthChild: 1` |
| `range` | Match a zero-based, half-open source range | `range: {start: {line: 0, column: 0}, end: {line: 0, column: 10}}` |

### Pattern forms

A string pattern directly represents the target structure:

```yaml
pattern: console.log($ARG)
```

Use an object pattern when parsing requires surrounding context or a selected node:

```yaml
pattern:
  context: class Example { $FIELD }
  selector: field_definition
  strictness: smart
```

Supported strictness values are `cst`, `smart`, `ast`, `relaxed`, `signature`, and `template`. The default is `smart`.

### Node kind

`kind` uses the language grammar's tree-sitter node name. Confirm uncertain kinds with `dump_syntax_tree` rather than guessing.

```yaml
kind: function_declaration
```

### Text regex

`regex` is applied to the entire matched node text and uses Rust regular-expression syntax. It is not a structural positive matcher by itself. Respect the runtime `forbid_regex_rules` capability reported by `get_server_info`.

### Child position

`nthChild` counts named children from one by default. It accepts an integer, an `An+B` expression such as `2n+1`, or an object with `position`, `reverse`, and `ofRule`.

### Source range

`range` contains `start` and `end` positions. Lines and columns are zero-based, the start is inclusive, and the end is exclusive.

## Relational rules

Relational rules constrain a target by surrounding syntax.

| Property | Relationship |
| --- | --- |
| `inside` | Target has a matching ancestor |
| `has` | Target has a matching descendant |
| `precedes` | Target appears before a matching node |
| `follows` | Target appears after a matching node |

Use `stopBy: end` for a deep search through the full relevant direction:

```yaml
rule:
  kind: function_declaration
  has:
    pattern: await $EXPR
    stopBy: end
```

The default `stopBy: neighbor` inspects only the immediate surrounding node. `stopBy` may instead contain a rule that terminates traversal when it matches. `inside` and `has` also accept `field`; `precedes` and `follows` do not.

```yaml
rule:
  pattern: console.log($$$ARGS)
  inside:
    kind: method_definition
    stopBy: end
```

## Composite rules

### Conjunction

`all` matches when every sub-rule matches and preserves evaluation order:

```yaml
rule:
  all:
    - kind: function_declaration
    - has:
        pattern: await $EXPR
        stopBy: end
```

### Alternatives

`any` matches when one sub-rule matches:

```yaml
rule:
  any:
    - pattern: console.log($$$ARGS)
    - pattern: console.warn($$$ARGS)
    - pattern: console.error($$$ARGS)
```

### Negation

`not` excludes a matching sub-rule:

```yaml
rule:
  all:
    - kind: function_declaration
    - has:
        pattern: await $EXPR
        stopBy: end
    - not:
        has:
          pattern: try { $$$BODY } catch ($ERROR) { $$$HANDLER }
          stopBy: end
```

### Utility rules

`matches` invokes a utility rule retained by an ast-grep configuration:

```yaml
rule:
  matches: my-utility-rule-id
```

Inline rules can use only utility rules made available by the effective inline configuration. Inspect `get_server_info` rather than assuming a mutable source configuration is active.

## Metavariables

| Form | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `$VAR` | One named node, captured |
| `$$VAR` | One unnamed node, captured |
| `$$$VAR` | Zero or more nodes, captured non-greedily |
| `$_VAR` | One named node, not captured |

Metavariable names use uppercase letters, digits, and underscores. `$META`, `$META_VAR`, and `$_` are valid; `$lower`, `$123`, and `$KEBAB-CASE` are not. A metavariable must occupy an entire AST node. Forms such as `obj.on$EVENT`, `"Hello $WORLD"`, and `a $OP b` are ordinary source text rather than metavariable patterns.

Repeated capturing metavariables require structural equality:

```yaml
pattern: $LEFT == $LEFT
```

Use a double-dollar metavariable for unnamed nodes such as operators:

```yaml
rule:
  kind: binary_expression
  has:
    field: operator
    pattern: $$OPERATOR
```

Use a triple-dollar metavariable for sequences:

```yaml
pattern: function $NAME($$$PARAMS) { $$$BODY }
```

## Debugging rules

When a rule does not match:

1. Confirm the language against `get_server_info.supported_language_ids` and inspect the target with `dump_syntax_tree` only if the search diagnostic is insufficient.
2. Replace a complex pattern with its expected `kind`.
3. Test the atomic target matcher by itself.
4. Add relational constraints one at a time.
5. Use `stopBy: end` when the intended relationship is not immediate.
6. Add composite constraints one at a time.
7. Confirm each metavariable occupies a complete node.
8. Test both positive and negative snippets.

Do not infer correctness from a single positive match. A useful rule must also reject near misses that represent the requested exclusions.

## Read-only previews

`find_code` accepts an optional `rewrite`. `find_code_by_rule` preserves configured `fix`, transformation, rewriter, replacement, and replacement-offset data. An empty replacement previews deletion. Treat all such data as proposals: ast-soleaux structural-search tools never write source files.

## Configured rules and tests

`scan_project_rules` runs only exact rule IDs retained in the immutable startup snapshot. Rule IDs, search paths, and globs are validated and bounded. `test_project_rules` runs only retained test suites and never updates snapshots or enters an interactive mode.

Do not replace these tools with raw ast-grep arguments. The MCP surface intentionally excludes arbitrary CLI flags, mutation, ignore bypasses, and configuration reloads.

## Result interpretation

Search responses contain bounded `matches`, `returned`, `truncated`, and `limit` fields. A truncated response proves only that more than the requested limit was observed. File paths are project-relative. Lines and columns are zero-based; UTF-8 byte offsets are zero-based; ranges are half-open.

Unknown upstream fields are retained in structured results. Preserve them when they carry metadata or preview information rather than narrowing a result to only familiar fields.

## Reference examples

Find console calls inside class methods:

```yaml
id: console-in-class-method
language: JavaScript
severity: info
message: console call in class method
rule:
  pattern: console.$METHOD($$$ARGS)
  inside:
    kind: method_definition
    stopBy: end
```

Find functions that await but lack a try statement:

```yaml
id: await-without-try
language: JavaScript
severity: warning
message: await without local try statement
rule:
  all:
    - kind: function_declaration
    - has:
        pattern: await $EXPR
        stopBy: end
    - not:
        has:
          kind: try_statement
          stopBy: end
```

Find calls with one or more arguments:

```yaml
id: nonempty-call
language: TypeScript
severity: info
message: call with arguments
rule:
  pattern: $CALLEE($FIRST, $$$REST)
```
